Here is the prompt: Write a single self-contained Python 3 script (intended to
run under Termux on Android, so the shebang points to the Termux Python path)
that inspects files in a directory tree, detects each file's true type and
extension, and reports or fixes mismatches between declared extensions and
detected content.
Use only the standard library as the core, with optional third-party detectors
that are imported defensively.
Requirements: 1. **Imports and optional dependencies**: Use `from __future__
import annotations`.
Standard library modules to use include argparse, concurrent.futures, logging,
mimetypes, os, re, shutil, subprocess, sys, tarfile, tokenize, zipfile,
dataclasses, io.StringIO, pathlib.Path, and typing (with TYPE_CHECKING guards
for collections.abc Iterator and Sequence, plus Any and Callable).
Attempt to import these optional detectors each inside its own try/except
Exception block, falling back to None on failure: `puremagic`, `magic`
(python-magic), and `filetype`.
Also attempt to import a helper module named `dh` exposing BIN_EXT, MIME2EXT,
SHEBANG_MAP, TXT_EXT, get_files, and is_binary, aliased with a `DH_` prefix; on
failure set each to None.
Code must work when none of the optional modules are present. 2. **Constant
tables**: - `ALIASES`: a dict[str, str] mapping equivalent extensions to a
canonical one (e.g. `.jpeg`→`.jpg`, `.tiff`→`.tif`, `.htm`→`.html`,
`.tgz`→`.tar.gz`). - `COMPOUND_EXTS`: a tuple of multi-part extensions treated
as a single unit (`.tar.gz`, `.tar.bz2`, `.tar.xz`, `.min.js`, `.min.css`). -
`MIME2EXT`: a dict[str, str | list[str]] mapping MIME types to one or more
candidate extensions.
Populate it with a reasonable set of common types (text, images, archives,
documents, audio/video, code).
Also define text and binary extension sets, and a shebang-to-extension map
(interpreter name → extension) used for extensionless scripts. 3. **Detection
pipeline**: For each file, determine its detected MIME type/extension using the
following priority order, using the first successful result: - Use
`DH_MIME2EXT`/`DH_SHEBANG_MAP`/`dh_is_binary` when the `dh` helper is available.
- Otherwise use puremagic, then python-magic, then filetype, then the stdlib
`mimetypes` module by filename. - Inspect the first bytes for shebang lines
(`#!`) to detect extensionless scripts and map the interpreter to an extension.
- For text files, use `tokenize` (with StringIO) to verify Python source
validity where relevant. - For archives, inspect content with `zipfile` and
`tarfile` (including compound `.tar.*` names) to confirm type rather than
trusting the extension. - Optionally use `subprocess` to invoke the `file`
command as a last resort if available, guarded by `shutil.which`. 4. **Extension
normalization**: Apply COMPOUND_EXTS first so `.tar.gz` and `.min.js` are not
split into `.gz`/`.js`.
Then map through ALIASES.
Compare the normalized declared extension with the normalized detected
extension(s) from MIME2EXT (which may be a list; any match counts as a match).
5. **Dataclass result**: Define a `@dataclass` (e.g. a file-result record)
holding at least the path, declared extension, detected extension, detected MIME
type, detector used, and a boolean or status for mismatch, plus an optional
error message. 6. **Traversal and concurrency**: Walk the target directory
recursively (or use `dh.get_files` when available).
Process files concurrently with `concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor`, with a
configurable worker count.
Skip symlinks by default, and skip hidden directories such as `.git` unless told
otherwise.
Use `Path` objects throughout. 7. **Command-line interface**: Use argparse with
options including: target path (file or directory, default current directory),
`--fix` / rename-on-mismatch (use `shutil.move` or `Path.rename`, never
overwrite existing files, and report collisions), `--dry-run` (default when
`--fix` not set, print intended actions only), `--workers N`, `--verbose` /
`--quiet` controlling logging level, and an option to restrict to certain
extensions or to output results as text or JSON. 8. **Logging**: Configure the
`logging` module in `main()` with level set from verbosity flags.
Log detector selection, skipped files, and errors at appropriate levels.
Print final summary counts (scanned, matched, mismatched, fixed, errors). 9.
**Error handling**: Wrap per-file work in try/except so one unreadable or
corrupt file never aborts the run; record the error in the result record.
Handle `PermissionError`, `OSError`, `zipfile.BadZipFile`, `tarfile.TarError`,
`UnicodeDecodeError`, and `tokenize.TokenError` specifically.
Exit with non-zero status for invalid arguments or a nonexistent target path;
return 0 on a completed run even if some files errored.
Catch `KeyboardInterrupt` in `main()` and exit cleanly with status 130. 10.
**Edge cases**: Empty files, files with no extension, files with multiple dots,
hidden files, symlinks, binary files misnamed as text, text files misnamed as
binary, files that disappear mid-scan, and very large files (read only a bounded
prefix, e.g. a few KB, for detection). 11. **Entry point**: Guard execution with
`if __name__ == "__main__":` and call `sys.exit(main())`, where `main()` returns
an integer exit status.
Use type hints throughout (compatible with `from __future__ import
annotations`), docstrings for public functions and the dataclass, and no
external network access.
Output a single complete, runnable script with these behaviors, using clear
function names and consistent style. ---
